# Architecture Rules

## Hexagonal Architecture

Project follows Hexagonal Architecture (Ports and Adapters) with three layers:

### Domain Layer (`domain/`)

- Contains business logic only
- NO external dependencies
- Defines service interfaces as TypeScript types
- Contains domain entities, commands, errors
- Exports via `domain/index.ts`

### Gateway Layer (`gateway/`)

- Implements domain service interfaces
- Handles external concerns (REST APIs, storage, etc.)
- Contains implementation scanners for feature flag switching
- Maps external data to domain entities

### Application Layer (`app/`)

- Expo Router file-based routing
- React components and pages
- Uses services via Service Registry
- No direct business logic

## Service Registry Pattern

- Services accessed via `useServiceRegistry()` hook or `getServiceRegistry()` function
- NEVER directly import gateway implementations
- Registry defined in `gateway/serviceRegistry.ts`
- All services registered in `createServiceRegistry()`

## Infrastructure Registry Pattern

- Infrastructure (REST client, storage) accessed via `getInfrastructureRegistry()`
- Defined in `gateway/infrastructureRegistry.ts`
- Provides `restClient` and `storageClient`
- Used by gateway implementations

## Feature Flag System

- Feature flags control mock vs real implementations
- Use `mockImplemSwitcher()` in injection scanners
- Pattern: Check feature flag, return mock or real implementation
- Feature flags defined in `domain/featureFlagKey/`
- Registry in `gateway/featureFlippingRegistry.ts`

## Dependency Injection

- Services injected via Service Registry
- Infrastructure injected via Infrastructure Registry
- Context providers wrap application
- No direct instantiation of services

## Project Structure Rules

- Domain layer: Pure business logic, no side effects
- Gateway layer: Implements domain interfaces, handles external I/O
- Application layer: UI and routing only
- Services MUST be accessed through registries
- Feature flags MUST be used for implementation switching
